<section class="pa-section">
  <div class="pa-tag">The current question</div>
  <p class="pa-lead">Our electricity bills. What has <em>being in the union</em> got to do with them?</p>
  <p class="pa-body">We generate our electricity from wind and water. This cheap green energy supplies every home in Scotland with electricity. We actually make more than we need, so the surplus is sent down south - for free of course. On some days we generate so much green energy that the National Grid tells us to switch our turbines off - Orkney's for example - because it cannot carry what we produce.</p>
  <p class="pa-body">Yet despite all this cheap green energy, our bills are among the highest in Europe. Why is that? And what's it got to do with the union?</p>
  <p class="pa-body">Everything, it turns out. Because it's directly related to how England generates its electricity.</p>
  <div class="pa-rule"></div>
  <p class="pa-body">Unlike Scotland, England still relies heavily on gas to generate its electricity. It is the single largest source of power south of the border. And because we're part of the UK-wide energy market, the price is set not by the cheapest source - wind - but by the most expensive  - gas.</p>
  <p class="pa-body">Burning gas is a very expensive way of generating electricity. Quite apart from the pollution it causes, the cost of gas is huge. Gas imports are also subject to trade disruption, like wars and blockades. Wind, on the other hand, is local and free. And will always be local and free. Forever.</p>
  <p class="pa-body">But we're in the union, and so we need to pay the price of England's gas-powered electricity generation. Which is the sole reason why our bills are so high.</p>
  <p class="pa-refrain">What is the point of Scotland <em>being in the union?</em></p>
